-- CySA+ (CS0-003) questions, part A: Domain 1 Security Operations (28q),
-- Domain 2 Vulnerability Management (26q).
-- Domain 2: Vulnerability Management
('cy-q-029','cysa',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$Why does a credentialed scan generally produce more accurate results than a non-credentialed scan?$q$,$q$["It runs faster","It logs in to inspect installed software, patches, and configs directly","It requires no network access","It never produces false positives"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Credentialed scans authenticate and read the system from inside - actual patch levels, versions, and settings - dramatically reducing false positives and negatives versus the outside-in non-credentialed view.$q$),
('cy-q-030','cysa',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$A fragile legacy ICS device crashes when scanned. Which scanning approach is safest?$q$,$q$["Aggressive active scanning","Passive scanning of network traffic","Credentialed active scanning","Repeated port scanning"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Passive scanning observes traffic without probing the device, avoiding the disruption that active scanning causes fragile OT/ICS systems. All active approaches risk crashing these devices.$q$),
('cy-q-031','cysa',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$Two vulnerabilities have CVSS 9.0. One is on an isolated lab box; the other is internet-facing and listed in CISA KEV. Which do you remediate first and why?$q$,$q$["The lab box, because CVSS is equal","The KEV-listed internet-facing one, because it is actively exploited and exposed","Neither, wait for vendor guidance","Both are equal priority"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Equal CVSS does not mean equal risk. The internet-facing, actively exploited (KEV) vulnerability is being used by attackers right now against an exposed asset - it wins prioritization decisively.$q$),
('cy-q-032','cysa',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$What does the CISA KEV catalog tell an analyst?$q$,$q$["The theoretical severity of a flaw","Which vulnerabilities are being actively exploited in the wild","The vendor patch schedule","The software license status"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$KEV lists vulnerabilities under active exploitation - a strong prioritization signal to patch now regardless of CVSS. CVSS gives theoretical severity; KEV gives real-world exploitation.$q$),
('cy-q-033','cysa',2,'2.2','mcq',3,$q$In CVSS base metrics, which combination represents the MOST easily exploited vulnerability?$q$,$q$["Local vector, high complexity, high privileges","Network vector, low complexity, no privileges, no user interaction","Physical vector, high privileges","Adjacent vector, user interaction required"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Network attack vector, low complexity, no privileges required, and no user interaction means anyone on the network can exploit it trivially and unattended - the highest exploitability profile.$q$),
('cy-q-034','cysa',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$What does EPSS add to vulnerability prioritization that CVSS does not?$q$,$q$["The vendor name","The probability the vulnerability will be exploited","The patch file size","The compliance requirement"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$EPSS estimates the likelihood of exploitation, complementing CVSS severity. Together they answer both "how bad if exploited" and "how likely to be exploited."$q$),
('cy-q-035','cysa',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$Which pairing is correct?$q$,$q$["CVE = weakness class; CWE = specific instance","CVE = specific vulnerability instance; CWE = weakness class","CVE and CWE are identical","CPE = severity score"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A CVE identifies a specific vulnerability instance; a CWE describes the underlying weakness class (e.g., CWE-89 SQL injection). CPE is platform naming, not a score.$q$),
('cy-q-036','cysa',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$A team wants to find vulnerabilities in source code before the application is ever deployed. Which testing type fits?$q$,$q$["DAST","SAST","Penetration testing of production","Fuzzing the live app"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$SAST analyzes source or binary statically, early in development, before deployment. DAST, production pen testing, and live fuzzing all require a running/deployed application.$q$),
('cy-q-037','cysa',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$Which testing method best finds runtime flaws like authentication bypass in a deployed web app?$q$,$q$["SAST","DAST","SCA","Code linting"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$DAST tests the running application from the outside, exercising real request/response behavior to find runtime issues like auth bypass. SAST reads code, SCA scans dependencies, linting checks style.$q$),
('cy-q-038','cysa',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$A build pipeline flags a known-vulnerable open-source library pulled in as a dependency. Which tool did this?$q$,$q$["SAST","Software Composition Analysis (SCA)","WAF","SIEM"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$SCA inventories third-party/open-source components and flags known vulnerabilities and license issues - the supply-chain layer. SAST scans your own code; WAF and SIEM are runtime/monitoring tools.$q$),
('cy-q-039','cysa',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$A critical patch cannot be applied for 90 days due to a vendor dependency. The risk owner documents acceptance and adds monitoring and segmentation. What is the segmentation?$q$,$q$["Remediation","A compensating control","A false positive","An exploit"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$When the fix (patch) isn't feasible yet, an alternative that reduces the same risk - here segmentation plus monitoring - is a compensating control. Remediation would be the patch itself.$q$),
('cy-q-040','cysa',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$Which is a legitimate inhibitor to immediate remediation that must be weighed?$q$,$q$["The vulnerability has a CVE ID","Patching would break a business-critical legacy application","The scanner is credentialed","The asset is inventoried"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Business process interruption, legacy application breakage, MOUs/SLAs, and degrading functionality are real remediation inhibitors. Having a CVE, using credentialed scans, or being inventoried are not obstacles.$q$),
('cy-q-041','cysa',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$A scanner reports a critical vulnerability, but manual review shows the affected service is not installed. What is this and the correct action?$q$,$q$["False negative; ignore it","False positive; validate and mark it accordingly","True positive; patch immediately","A zero-day; report to CISA"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A reported flaw that doesn't actually apply is a false positive; the analyst validates and dispositions it to avoid wasted remediation. A false negative is a missed real flaw.$q$),
('cy-q-042','cysa',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$An attacker submits ' OR '1'='1 into a login field and gains access. Which weakness class (CWE) is this?$q$,$q$["Cross-site scripting","SQL injection","Buffer overflow","SSRF"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Injecting SQL syntax to alter a query's logic is SQL injection (CWE-89). XSS injects browser script, buffer overflow corrupts memory, and SSRF coerces server-side requests.$q$),
('cy-q-043','cysa',2,'2.5','mcq',3,$q$A web app fetches a URL supplied by the user; an attacker points it at the cloud metadata endpoint and retrieves IAM credentials. Which vulnerability is this?$q$,$q$["Cross-site scripting","Server-Side Request Forgery","Directory traversal","Insecure deserialization"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$SSRF coerces the server into making attacker-chosen requests; in cloud, reaching the instance metadata service exposes temporary IAM credentials. The other flaws don't produce this cloud-credential path.$q$),
('cy-q-044','cysa',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$Which OWASP Top 10 category covers failures that let a user access another user''s data by changing an ID in the URL?$q$,$q$["Cryptographic failures","Broken access control","Security misconfiguration","Vulnerable components"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Insecure direct object references and missing authorization checks fall under broken access control - the ability to reach resources you shouldn't. It is consistently the top OWASP category.$q$),
('cy-q-045','cysa',2,'2.1','ordering',2,$q$Place the vulnerability management lifecycle steps in order.$q$,$q$["Identification (scan/discover assets)","Analysis and prioritization","Remediation","Validation (rescan)"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$The lifecycle runs identification (discover assets and flaws) to analysis/prioritization (validate, rank by risk) to remediation (patch/mitigate) to validation (rescan to confirm), feeding continuous reporting.$q$),
('cy-q-046','cysa',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$What is insecure deserialization?$q$,$q$["Encrypting data with a weak cipher","Processing untrusted serialized data, potentially leading to code execution","Storing passwords in plaintext","Allowing weak TLS versions"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Deserializing attacker-controlled data without validation can lead to remote code execution or object injection. The other options describe cryptographic or storage weaknesses, not deserialization.$q$),
('cy-q-047','cysa',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$Which technique feeds malformed or random inputs to an application to trigger crashes that reveal vulnerabilities?$q$,$q$["Fuzzing","Threat modeling","Log correlation","Sinkholing"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Fuzzing bombards an application with malformed/random input to surface crashes and edge-case flaws. Threat modeling is a design activity; log correlation and sinkholing are operational/detection techniques.$q$),
('cy-q-048','cysa',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$After applying a patch to remediate a critical vulnerability, what MUST occur before the finding is closed?$q$,$q$["Update the asset inventory only","Rescan/validate to confirm the fix worked","Notify the vendor","Rotate all passwords"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Validation via rescan confirms the remediation actually applied and the exposure is gone - patches fail silently more often than expected. Only then is the finding legitimately closed.$q$),
('cy-q-049','cysa',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$What does asset criticality drive in a vulnerability management program?$q$,$q$["The color of dashboards","Scan frequency, prioritization, and acceptable scan intrusiveness","The choice of programming language","The office location"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$How essential an asset is determines how often it's scanned, how findings are prioritized, and whether disruptive active scanning is tolerable. It does not affect cosmetic or unrelated factors.$q$),
('cy-q-050','cysa',2,'2.2','mcq',3,$q$Why can a set of individually low-severity vulnerabilities represent a high real-world risk?$q$,$q$["Low-severity flaws are always false positives","They can be chained into a single high-impact attack path","Scanners double-count them","CVSS multiplies them automatically"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Vulnerability chaining combines several low-severity flaws (e.g., info disclosure + weak auth + SSRF) into a serious compromise - which is why per-CVE scores can understate risk. The other statements are false.$q$),
('cy-q-051','cysa',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$Scanning Terraform templates for misconfigurations before deployment is an example of what?$q$,$q$["DAST","Infrastructure-as-Code (IaC) scanning","Penetration testing","Fuzzing"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$IaC scanning inspects infrastructure templates (Terraform, CloudFormation) for insecure settings before they are provisioned - shifting security left. DAST/pen testing/fuzzing target running systems.$q$),
('cy-q-052','cysa',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$A request for /app?file=../../../../etc/passwd returns the system password file. Which vulnerability is this?$q$,$q$["SQL injection","Directory traversal","XSS","CSRF"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The ../ sequences escape the intended directory to read arbitrary files - directory (path) traversal. Mitigate with input validation/canonicalization and least-privilege file permissions.$q$),
('cy-q-053','cysa',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$Which CVSS metric group reflects characteristics that change over time, such as exploit code maturity?$q$,$q$["Base","Temporal","Environmental","Physical"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The Temporal group captures time-varying factors (exploit maturity, remediation level, report confidence). Base is intrinsic and constant; Environmental adjusts for the specific organization.$q$),
('cy-q-054','cysa',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$Which sequence correctly distinguishes the response options for a discovered vulnerability?$q$,$q$["Remediate = reduce impact; Mitigate = fully fix; Compensating = ignore","Remediate = fully fix; Mitigate = reduce likelihood/impact; Compensating = alternative when fix not feasible","All three mean the same thing","Compensating = fully fix; Remediate = ignore"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Remediation fully resolves (patch/upgrade); mitigation reduces likelihood or impact; a compensating control substitutes an alternative safeguard when the primary fix isn't feasible.$q$);
