insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('cy-q-001','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$A SOC analyst sees a workstation making an outbound HTTPS connection of nearly identical size to the same external IP every 60 seconds, around the clock. What does this MOST likely indicate?$q$,$q$["Normal software update checking","C2 beaconing from a compromised host","A misconfigured NTP client","Legitimate cloud backup"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Regular, uniform-size connections to one external host at a fixed interval is the signature of command-and-control beaconing. Legitimate traffic (updates, backups, NTP) is bursty, varied in size, and not perfectly periodic around the clock.$q$),
('cy-q-002','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$Which capability lets an analyst remotely isolate a compromised endpoint from the network while preserving it for investigation?$q$,$q$["SIEM correlation","EDR host isolation","DNS sinkhole","WAF blocking"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$EDR provides host isolation (network quarantine) plus process termination and rollback while keeping the machine available for forensics. A SIEM detects but doesn't act on endpoints; sinkholes redirect DNS; WAFs filter web traffic.$q$),
('cy-q-003','cysa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$An analyst wants to map observed attacker behavior to a standardized matrix of tactics and techniques. Which framework fits BEST?$q$,$q$["OWASP Top 10","MITRE ATT&CK","CVSS","NIST 800-53"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$MITRE ATT&CK catalogs adversary tactics (goals) and techniques (methods) from real intrusions - purpose-built for mapping observed behavior. OWASP ranks web app risks, CVSS scores severity, and NIST 800-53 is a controls catalog.$q$),
('cy-q-003b','cysa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$According to the pyramid of pain, denying which indicator causes an attacker the MOST difficulty?$q$,$q$["File hashes","IP addresses","Domain names","TTPs"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,$q$Tactics, techniques, and procedures sit at the top of the pyramid - forcing an attacker to change how they operate is far costlier than swapping an easily regenerated hash, IP, or domain.$q$),
('cy-q-004','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$An analyst reviewing a phishing email wants to determine the true originating server. Which header provides the hop-by-hop path?$q$,$q$["Subject","Received","Reply-To","Content-Type"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The Received headers record each mail server hop in reverse order, revealing the true originating IP. Reply-To can reveal spoofing intent but not the path; Subject and Content-Type are not routing data.$q$),
('cy-q-005','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',1,$q$Which data source provides conversation metadata (source, destination, ports, bytes) without capturing payloads?$q$,$q$["Full packet capture","NetFlow","Memory dump","Disk image"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$NetFlow records flow metadata efficiently at scale - ideal for spotting anomalies and beaconing. Full packet capture includes payloads (heavy storage); memory dumps and disk images are host forensic artifacts.$q$),
('cy-q-006','cysa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$A threat intel team receives machine-readable indicators via a standardized transport protocol. Which pair describes the language and transport?$q$,$q$["YARA and Snort","STIX and TAXII","CVE and CVSS","SAML and OAuth"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$STIX is the structured language for threat intelligence; TAXII is the protocol that transports it. YARA/Snort are detection rule formats, CVE/CVSS describe vulnerabilities, and SAML/OAuth are auth standards.$q$),
('cy-q-007','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$An analyst suspects fileless malware. Which artifact is MOST likely to contain the evidence?$q$,$q$["Disk file listing","Memory (RAM)","Firewall config","Backup tapes"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Fileless malware runs in memory using legitimate tools and leaves little on disk. RAM captures the running process, injected code, and network state - which vanish at power-off. Disk listings and backups won't show it.$q$),
('cy-q-008','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$Which technique redirects lookups for a known-malicious domain to a controlled address to sever C2 and identify infected hosts?$q$,$q$["Port mirroring","DNS sinkholing","MAC filtering","TLS inspection"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$DNS sinkholing points malicious domains at a controlled IP - callbacks fail and the sinkhole logs which internal hosts tried to reach the domain. Port mirroring copies traffic; MAC filtering controls device access; TLS inspection decrypts sessions.$q$),
('cy-q-009','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',3,$q$A SOC is overwhelmed by alerts and analysts are ignoring most of them. What is the BEST first corrective action?$q$,$q$["Disable the SIEM to reduce noise","Tune correlation rules and thresholds to reduce false positives","Hire more analysts immediately","Forward all alerts to email"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Alert fatigue is solved by tuning - refining rules, thresholds, and suppression so real signals surface. Disabling detection is reckless, headcount doesn't fix noise, and email routing worsens it.$q$),
('cy-q-010','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$An analyst detonates a suspicious attachment in an isolated environment to observe its file, registry, and network behavior. What is this?$q$,$q$["Static analysis","Sandbox (dynamic) analysis","Fuzzing","Code review"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Executing malware in an isolated sandbox to observe runtime behavior is dynamic analysis. Static analysis inspects the file without running it; fuzzing feeds malformed input to find bugs; code review reads source.$q$),
('cy-q-011','cysa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$Which describes "living off the land" techniques?$q$,$q$["Using zero-day exploits exclusively","Abusing legitimate built-in tools like PowerShell and WMI","Physically stealing hardware","Encrypting files for ransom"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Living off the land means using legitimate system tools already present (PowerShell, WMI, PsExec) to blend in and evade detection, minimizing malware on disk. The other options describe different attack types.$q$),
('cy-q-012','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$Which detection approach flags a user account suddenly downloading gigabytes at 3 a.m. from a new location?$q$,$q$["Signature-based IDS","UEBA (behavioral analytics)","Antivirus scanning","Port scanning"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$UEBA baselines normal behavior and flags deviations - odd hours, unusual volume, new locations - catching compromised or insider accounts. Signature IDS and AV match known patterns, not behavioral anomalies.$q$),
('cy-q-013','cysa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$An analyst tracks an intrusion using four linked vertices: adversary, capability, infrastructure, and victim. Which model is this?$q$,$q$["Cyber Kill Chain","Diamond Model","STRIDE","OWASP"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis connects adversary, capability, infrastructure, and victim to understand and correlate intrusions into campaigns. The Kill Chain is linear stages; STRIDE is threat modeling; OWASP ranks app risks.$q$),
('cy-q-014','cysa',1,'1.2','ordering',2,$q$Place the Cyber Kill Chain stages in order.$q$,$q$["Reconnaissance","Weaponization","Delivery","Exploitation","Installation","Command & Control","Actions on Objectives"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]$q$::jsonb,$q$The Lockheed Martin Cyber Kill Chain proceeds recon (target research) to weaponization (build payload) to delivery to exploitation to installation (persistence) to C2 to actions on objectives. Disrupting any earlier stage stops the later ones.$q$),
('cy-q-015','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$What is the security value of correlating events across multiple log sources rather than reviewing them individually?$q$,$q$["It reduces storage costs","It links related events into higher-fidelity detections","It eliminates the need for retention","It replaces endpoint protection"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Correlation ties together events (failed logins + new admin account + data transfer) that are meaningless alone but together indicate a real incident - the core value a SIEM adds over raw log storage.$q$),
('cy-q-016','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',3,$q$An analyst wants durable detections that survive an attacker changing their infrastructure. Detections built on which indicator type are MOST durable?$q$,$q$["File hashes","IP addresses","Behavioral TTPs","Domain names"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$TTP-based detections target how an attacker operates, which is expensive for them to change - unlike hashes, IPs, and domains that are trivially rotated. This is the top of the pyramid of pain.$q$),
('cy-q-017','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$Which distinguishes east-west traffic monitoring from north-south?$q$,$q$["East-west is encrypted; north-south is not","East-west is internal host-to-host; north-south crosses the perimeter","East-west is wireless only","They are identical"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$East-west is lateral, internal host-to-host traffic (key to detecting lateral movement); north-south crosses the network boundary. Encryption and medium are unrelated to the distinction.$q$),
('cy-q-018','cysa',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$What is the PRIMARY purpose of proactive threat hunting?$q$,$q$["Respond to triggered alerts faster","Search for compromises that evaded existing detections","Replace the vulnerability scanner","Generate compliance reports"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Threat hunting is hypothesis-driven, proactive searching for undetected intrusions - it assumes breach and looks for evidence the alerting stack missed. It complements, not replaces, alerting and scanning.$q$),
('cy-q-019','cysa',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$Which describes structured threat data versus unstructured?$q$,$q$["Structured is encrypted; unstructured is plaintext","Structured is machine-readable (STIX); unstructured is free-text reports","Structured is internal; unstructured is external","There is no difference"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Structured threat data is standardized and machine-readable (STIX, CSV feeds) for automated ingestion; unstructured is free-text (blogs, PDF reports) needing human or NLP interpretation. Encryption and origin are irrelevant.$q$),
('cy-q-020','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$A honeytoken (fake credential) is used in production. What makes it a high-fidelity alert?$q$,$q$["It encrypts sensitive data","Any use of it is inherently illegitimate","It speeds up authentication","It replaces MFA"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A honeytoken has no legitimate purpose, so any use signals an intruder with near-zero false positives. It doesn't protect data, speed auth, or replace MFA.$q$),
('cy-q-021','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',3,$q$SIEM logs show a service account authenticating to dozens of hosts in minutes using valid credentials. Which activity does this MOST likely represent?$q$,$q$["Normal backup job","Lateral movement","DNS tunneling","A denial-of-service attack"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Rapid authentication across many hosts with valid credentials is classic lateral movement - an attacker using stolen credentials to spread. Backups target specific systems predictably; DNS tunneling and DoS present differently.$q$),
('cy-q-022','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$Which tool inspects HTTP/S requests to block SQL injection and cross-site scripting against a web application?$q$,$q$["Network firewall","Web Application Firewall","IDS","Load balancer"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A WAF operates at Layer 7, inspecting HTTP/S for app-layer attacks like SQLi and XSS that network firewalls (Layer 3/4) cannot see. An IDS detects but is not app-aware by default; a load balancer distributes traffic.$q$),
('cy-q-023','cysa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$In MITRE ATT&CK, what is the relationship between a tactic and a technique?$q$,$q$["A tactic is a tool; a technique is a person","A tactic is the goal; a technique is how it is achieved","They are synonyms","A technique contains many tactics"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A tactic is the adversary's objective (e.g., Persistence); a technique is a specific method to achieve it (e.g., scheduled task). Sub-techniques refine techniques further.$q$),
('cy-q-024','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$Which is the BEST use of OSINT during an investigation?$q$,$q$["Decrypting captured traffic","Enriching an indicator with public context (WHOIS, reputation, breach data)","Isolating an endpoint","Rebuilding a server"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$OSINT enriches indicators using public sources - WHOIS registration, IP/domain reputation, breach dumps - to add context. It doesn't decrypt traffic or perform containment/recovery actions.$q$),
('cy-q-025','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',3,$q$An analyst needs to confirm whether data actually left the network, not just that a large connection occurred. Which source is required?$q$,$q$["NetFlow metadata","Full packet capture","Firewall allow/deny logs","Asset inventory"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Confirming exfiltration content requires full packet capture (payloads), assuming it was captured and is decryptable. NetFlow and firewall logs show that a transfer occurred and its size, but not what was sent.$q$),
('cy-q-026','cysa',1,'1.1','matching',2,$q$Match each tool to its primary function.$q$,$q${"left":["SIEM","SOAR","EDR","DLP"],"right":["Aggregate and correlate logs","Automate response playbooks","Detect and respond on endpoints","Prevent sensitive data exfiltration"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$A SIEM aggregates and correlates logs; SOAR automates response playbooks across tools; EDR provides endpoint detection and response; DLP detects and blocks sensitive data leaving the org.$q$),
('cy-q-027','cysa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$Which threat intelligence type describes the strategic who and why behind attacks for executive decision-making?$q$,$q$["Operational intelligence","Tactical intelligence","Strategic intelligence","Technical intelligence"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Strategic intelligence is high-level (threat landscape, actor motivations, geopolitical trends) for leadership decisions. Tactical/technical intel covers TTPs and IOCs; operational covers specific impending campaigns.$q$),
('cy-q-028','cysa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$What does enabling PowerShell script block logging primarily help an analyst detect?$q$,$q$["Physical intrusions","Fileless and living-off-the-land attacks using PowerShell","Weak Wi-Fi passwords","Expired certificates"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Script block logging records the actual PowerShell executed, exposing fileless/LOTL attacks that abuse PowerShell in memory. It has nothing to do with physical, Wi-Fi, or certificate issues.$q$),
